@@s001 How the oral exam works
@@s002 <span>Exam</span>
@@s003 How the oral exam works
@@s004 A <b>30-minute oral</b>, with no preparation time, between <b>21 and 29 January 2027</b>. Questions on <b>any part</b> of the programme. Approved assignments are required to be admitted. Below is everything the official documents say.
@@s005 On one screen
@@s006 Type
@@s007 <b>Oral</b>, variant <b>B22</b>: no preparation time
@@s008 Duration
@@s009 <b>30 minutes</b>, including the decision on the grade
@@s010 When
@@s011 one of the days 21, 22, 25, 26, 27, 28, 29 Jan 2027, between 9:00 and 21:00
@@s012 Grade
@@s013 Danish 7-point scale, <b>external examiner</b>
@@s014 Questions
@@s015 "about <b>any part of the syllabus</b>"
@@s016 Artificial intelligence
@@s017 <b>forbidden</b> in the exam in any form
@@s018 Admission
@@s019 at least <b>5 assignments out of 6</b> approved
@@s020 Without admission
@@s021 grade <b>NA</b> and one exam attempt used up
@@s022 Re-examination
@@s023 same format: B22 oral of 30 minutes
@@s024 Platform
@@s025 WISEflow (login with the ITU account)
@@s026 Two numbers that do not match
@@s027 The official description says "80% of the hand-ins must be approved", with a final hand-in to recover those not approved; LearnIT and the slides of lecture 1 say "at least 5 assignments" out of 6. They are the same threshold (5/6 ≈ 83%). Slide 6 talks about <b>13</b> sets of exercises, LearnIT about <b>12</b>: the number of assignments counts, and it is 6 in both.
@@s028 What to expect
@@s029 <b>No preparation</b>: the question arrives and you answer at once. Train yourself to reason <b>out loud</b>, in English, on small examples (<a href="come-rispondere.html">how to answer</a>).
@@s030 The slides of lecture 1: "<b>Oral feedback sessions aim at preparing you for the exam</b>". The feedback sessions on the assignments are the dress rehearsal: same type of questions, same vocabulary.
@@s031 The course stresses <b>reasoning about the correctness</b> of concurrent programs: interleaving, happens-before, thread-safety, linearizability. From the description: "the course places emphasis on how to reason about the correctness of concurrent programs".
@@s032 The <b>challenging</b> exercises "are meant for students aiming at high grades in the exam": they are not needed to pass the assignments, but they are the level of the high grades.
@@s033 <b>Past exams</b>: being an oral, none are published. The training material is the questions in the blue boxes of the slides and the exercises: <a href="allenamento.html">training questions</a>.
@@s034 Still to be published
@@s035 The <b>Examination Syllabus</b> (the compulsory reading for the exam) comes out on LearnIT <b>by the last lecture</b>. Lecture 14 (30 November) is dedicated to preparing for the exam. When they come out, this page must be updated.
@@s036 Learning objectives
@@s037 From the official description: after the course you must be able to
@@s038 Verb
@@s039 What
@@s040 <b>ANALYSE</b> + <b>RELATE</b>
@@s041 the correctness of concurrent Java software, relating it to the Java memory model
@@s042 <b>ANALYSE</b>
@@s043 the performance of concurrent Java software
@@s044 <b>APPLY</b> + <b>CONSTRUCT</b>
@@s045 threads, locks, final and volatile fields, concurrent collections to build correct and fast software
@@s046 <b>USE</b>
@@s047 tools to test and analyse concurrency problems
@@s048 <b>COMPARE</b>
@@s049 shared memory and message passing
@@s050 <b>APPLY</b> + <b>CONSTRUCT</b>
@@s051 the actor model with Erlang
@@s052 <b>DESCRIBE</b> + <b>DISCUSS</b>
@@s053 the differences between multicore CPUs and GPUs and the consequences on performance
